( n \\):\n  \\[\n  n^3 \\geq 100 \\implies n \\geq \\sqrt[3]{100} \\approx 4.64\n  \\]\n  Since \\( n \\) must be an integer, the smallest \\( n \\) is 5.\n\n- For the largest \\( n \\):\n  \\[\n  n^3 \\leq 999 \\implies n \\leq \\sqrt[3]{999} \\approx 9.997\n  \\]\n  Since \\( n \\) must be an integer, the largest \\( n \\) is 9.\n\nThus, \\( n \\) ranges from 5 to 9.\n\n### Step 3: List the three-digit cubes of integers from 5 to 9\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n5^3 &= 125, \\\\\n6^3 &= 216, \\\\\n7^3 &= 343, \\\\\n8^3 &= 512, \\\\\n9^3 &= 729.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\n### Step 4: Determine which of these cubes are cubes of squares or cubes\n- \\( 125 = 5^3 \\). \\( 5 \\) is neither a square nor a cube.\n- \\( 216 = 6^3 \\). \\( 6 \\) is neither a square nor a cube.\n- \\( 343 = 7^3 \\). \\( 7 \\) is neither a square nor a cube.\n- \\( 512 = 8^3 \\). \\( 8 = 2^3 \\) is a cube.\n- \\( 729 = 9^3 \\). \\( 9 = 3^2 \\) is a square.\n\n### Step 5: Sum the cubes that are cubes of squares or cubes\nThe only numbers that meet the criteria are \\( 512 \\) and \\( 729 \\).\n\n\\[\n512 + 729 = 1241\n\\]\n\n### Final Answer\n\\[\n\\boxed{1241}\n\\]","id":"number_theory:182"}
